       SECTION 1: WHAT TIME ZONE DOES NEW YORK USE?
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <section id="what-timezone-newyork" class="content-section reveal" aria-label="What Time Zone Does New York Use?">
    <h2>1. What Time Zone Does New York Use?</h2>
    <p>
      New York is the commercial, financial, and cultural hub of the United States. 
      Because of its geographical position on the East Coast of North America, New York operates in the Eastern Time Zone (ET). 
      This zone is one of the most populated and economically active timezone corridors in the world, covering major cities like Washington D.C., Boston, Miami, Toronto, and Montreal.
    </p>

    <!-- Visual Asset 1: New York Skyline with Clock Illustration -->
    <figure style="margin: 2.5rem 0; text-align: center; border: 1px solid var(--line); padding: 1.5rem; border-radius: 12px; background: var(--panel);">
      <img 
        src="/assets/new-york-skyline-clock.webp" 
        alt="New York Skyline with Clock showing Eastern Time" 
        title="New York Skyline with Clock Illustration" 
        loading="lazy" 
        decoding="async" 
        width="800" 
        height="450" 
        style="width: 100%; height: auto; border-radius: 8px;"
      />
      <figcaption class="infographic-caption">
        <strong>Figure 1: New York Skyline Time Reference.</strong> Visual representation of New York's skyline coupled with the Eastern Time (ET) indicator, representing standard (EST) and daylight (EDT) offsets.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>
    
    <div class="tip-box">
      <strong>Pro Tip:</strong> Eastern Time is the default timezone reference for U.S. federal government announcements, national television networks, and corporate contracts. 
      When a national event is scheduled in the United States, it is almost always announced in Eastern Time.
    </div>

    <p>
      Before the late 19th century, New York set its clocks by local mean time calculated from the meridian of the local city hall observatory. 
      This meant that clocks in Philadelphia were several minutes behind clocks in New York. 
      In 1883, railway companies standardized time by dividing the continent into four standard railway corridors, leading to the adoption of Eastern Standard Time. 
      To understand this concept further, read our guides on <a href="/gmt-vs-utc/">GMT vs UTC</a> and <a href="/utc-vs-est/">UTC vs EST</a>.
    </p>

    <!-- Visual Asset 2: New York Time Zone Map -->
    <figure style="margin: 2.5rem 0; text-align: center; border: 1px solid var(--line); padding: 1.5rem; border-radius: 12px; background: var(--panel);">
