      </figcaption>
    </figure>
  </section>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       SECTION 2: EST VS EDT IN NEW YORK
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <section id="est-vs-edt" class="content-section reveal" aria-label="EST vs EDT in New York">
    <h2>2. EST vs. EDT in New York</h2>
    <p>
      The term "Eastern Time" (ET) is a generic label that covers two seasonal offsets observed in New York:
    </p>

    <!-- EST -->
    <article class="method-card" style="margin-bottom: 2rem;">
      <h3>Eastern Standard Time (EST)</h3>
      <p>
        <strong>Offset:</strong> UTC-5  
        <br /><strong>Observed:</strong> From the first Sunday of November until the second Sunday of March.  
        <br /><strong>Characteristics:</strong> This is New York's winter standard time, when clocks are set to their natural geographical offset. 
        Sunrise and sunset occur earlier in the day. 
        For detailed background, read our <a href="/what-is-est/">What is EST?</a> guide.
      </p>
    </article>

    <!-- EDT -->
    <article class="method-card" style="margin-bottom: 2rem;">
      <h3>Eastern Daylight Time (EDT)</h3>
      <p>
        <strong>Offset:</strong> UTC-4  
        <br /><strong>Observed:</strong> From the second Sunday of March until the first Sunday of November.  
        <br /><strong>Characteristics:</strong> This is New York's summer daylight saving time. 
        Clocks are set one hour ahead of standard time to extend evening daylight. 
        For comparisons, read our guide on <a href="/est-vs-edt/">EST vs EDT</a>.
      </p>
    </article>

    <!-- Visual Asset 3: EST vs EDT Timeline -->
    <figure style="margin: 2.5rem 0; text-align: center; border: 1px solid var(--line); padding: 1.5rem; border-radius: 12px; background: var(--panel);">
      <img 
        src="/assets/new-york-est-edt-timeline.webp" 
