        <strong>Figure 6: New York UTC Offsets.</strong> Infographic comparing New York's standard winter offset (UTC-5) and summer daylight saving offset (UTC-4) relative to the Coordinated Universal Time (UTC) baseline.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>
  </section>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       SECTION 3: CURRENT TIME SYSTEM IN NEW YORK
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <section id="current-time-system" class="content-section reveal" aria-label="Current Time System in New York">
    <h2>3. Current Time System in New York</h2>
    <p>
      In New York, the annual cycle of clock changes is regulated under federal law by the Uniform Time Act of 1966. 
      The transitions occur at 2:00 a.m. local time:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>The Spring Transition:</strong> On the second Sunday of March, at 2:00 a.m. local standard time, clocks skip forward by one hour to 3:00 a.m. daylight time. 
        This is referred to as "springing forward."
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>The Autumn Transition:</strong> On the first Sunday of November, at 2:00 a.m. local daylight time, clocks fall back by one hour to 1:00 a.m. standard time. 
        This is referred to as "falling back."
      </li>
    </ul>

    <!-- Visual Asset 10: New York Annual Clock Timeline -->
    <figure style="margin: 2.5rem 0; text-align: center; border: 1px solid var(--line); padding: 1.5rem; border-radius: 12px; background: var(--panel);">
