  <section id="programming-tech" class="content-section reveal" aria-label="Programming &amp; Technology Guidelines">
    <h2>7. Programming &amp; Technology Guidelines</h2>
    <p>
      In software engineering and cloud databases, hardcoding New York's time offsets (like -5 or -4) is considered a bad practice. 
      Offsets change seasonally and can be modified by federal legislation.
    </p>

    <!-- Visual Asset 9: Programming & UTC Workflow -->
    <figure style="margin: 2.5rem 0; text-align: center; border: 1px solid var(--line); padding: 1.5rem; border-radius: 12px; background: var(--panel);">
      <img 
        src="/assets/new-york-programming-utc-workflow.webp" 
        alt="Database storage to client rendering workflow for America/New_York location ID" 
        title="Programming & UTC Workflow" 
        loading="lazy" 
        decoding="async" 
        width="800" 
        height="400" 
        style="width: 100%; height: auto; border-radius: 8px;"
      />
      <figcaption class="infographic-caption">
        <strong>Figure 9: Developer Timezone Database Workflow.</strong> Diagram demonstrating the best practice of storing timezone-agnostic Coordinated Universal Time (UTC) in databases and translating to `America/New_York` at the client UI layer.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>

    <h3>1. Use IANA Identifiers</h3>
    <p>
      Always reference New York's official location name in the IANA Time Zone Database: **`America/New_York`**. 
      This identifier ensures your system automatically adapts to standard (EST) and daylight (EDT) transition schedules.
    </p>

    <h3>2. Storing Dates in UTC</h3>
    <p>
      Ensure your servers and databases store timestamps in Coordinated Universal Time (UTC) using ISO 8601 formatting: `YYYY-MM-DDTHH:MM:SSZ`. 
      Local UI layers should parse these timestamps and render them to the user using local Eastern Time formatting.
    </p>

    <h3>3. JavaScript Formatting Example</h3>
    <p>
      Use the native JavaScript `Intl.DateTimeFormat` API to render a database date object into New York local time:
    </p>

    <pre style="background: var(--panel); padding: 1.5rem; border-radius: 8px; border: 1px solid var(--line); overflow-x: auto; color: var(--text); font-family: monospace;">{`const date = new Date('2026-07-15T12:00:00Z');
const formatter = new Intl.DateTimeFormat('en-US', {
  timeZone: 'America/New_York',
  dateStyle: 'medium',
  timeStyle: 'long'
});
console.log(formatter.format(date)); 
// Output will render in New York local time (EST or EDT)`}</pre>
  </section>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       SECTION 8: COMMON MISTAKES TO AVOID
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <section id="common-mistakes" class="content-section reveal" aria-label="Common Time Zone Mistakes to Avoid">
    <h2>8. Common Time Zone Mistakes to Avoid</h2>
    <p>
      Avoid these frequent errors when coordinating schedules, travel, or coding projects involving New York:
    </p>
    <ol>
      <li>
        <strong>Confusing EST with EDT:</strong> EST is UTC-5 (winter), and EDT is UTC-4 (summer). 
        Do not use EST in summer communications, as it represents a different offset.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Ignoring the March/November Transition Gap:</strong> The US transitions to DST on different dates compared to Europe and South America. 
        For example, London transitions to BST several weeks after New York transitions to EDT, creating a brief period where the standard 5-hour time gap shifts to 4 hours.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Neglecting Local Travel Details:</strong> Flight arrival times are always printed in local local times. 
        Forgetting to adjust your clocks to Eastern Time upon landing can cause you to miss connection windows.
      </li>
    </ol>
  </section>

